            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/scheduling/work-centers","/parts"]""",
            Tags = """["manufacturing","work-centers","routing","operations","setup"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Work Centers and Routings\n\nBefore Forge can schedule a job, cost it, or tell you where it is on the floor, it needs two things from you: a list of the places work happens, and a description of the steps each made part goes through. Those are **work centers** and **routings**. Set them up once and everything downstream — the schedule, the dispatch list, operation time tracking, job costing, OEE — uses them.\n\n### Work centers\n\nA work center is a machine, a cell, a bench or a department that takes work: Saw, CNC-1, Weld, Paint Line, Assembly. You manage them on the **Work Centers** tab of the Scheduling page.\n\nEach work center carries a handful of numbers the rest of Forge leans on:\n\n- **Daily Capacity (hours)** — how many hours of work it can take in a day. The scheduler uses this to decide how much it can load onto the center before pushing work to the next day.\n- **Efficiency %** — how much of that capacity you actually get. A center that runs at 85 percent turns 8 hours of capacity into about 6.8 hours of real output.\n- **Number of Machines** — parallel machines that can run at the same time. Two identical presses on one work center double its throughput.\n- **Labor Cost / Hour** and **Burden Rate / Hour** — what an hour on this center costs in wages and in overhead. These feed the estimated cost on every job routed through it, unless you switch on departmental overhead rates in Costing.\n- **Assigned Asset** — optional link to the physical machine in Assets, so downtime and maintenance on the machine connect to the work center.\n- **Active** — turn a center off when it is retired or down for the season. Inactive centers stop appearing in routing and scheduling choices but keep their history.\n\nKeep the list honest. A work center that exists in Forge but not on the floor makes the schedule lie; a real bottleneck missing from Forge is invisible to the scheduler.\n\n### Routings\n\nA routing is the ordered list of operations needed to make a part. It lives on the **Routing** tab of the part, not on the job, so every job for that part starts from the same steps. Open a part you make and you will see its operations as a list of cards, or switch to **Flow** to see them as a diagram.\n\nEach operation has:\n\n- **Step number** — the order it runs in. Drag the cards to reorder; Forge renumbers for you.\n- **Title** and **Instructions** — what the operator does. The instructions show up wherever the operation is worked, so write them for the person at the machine.\n- **Work center** — where it runs. You can create a new work center right from the dialog if one is missing.\n- **Estimated time** — hours, minutes and, for very fast cycles, milliseconds. This is per operation and is the basis for scheduling, labor estimates and the efficiency numbers on the job.\n- **Materials** — which BOM lines this step consumes. Add the bill of materials to the part first, then assign lines to the operations that use them.\n- **QC Checkpoint** — flag the step as an inspection point and record the pass/fail criteria.\n- **Subcontract Operation** — the step happens at an outside vendor. Pick the vendor and enter the turn time in days; the schedule allows for the round trip and the job cost picks up the subcontract charge.\n- **References Operation** — point at another operation when a step reuses an existing one.\n\nParts you buy do not need a routing. Parts you make need at least one step before the part can be promoted to active.\n\n### How the two connect\n\nWhen a job is created for a part, its routing becomes the job's operations, and each operation lands on its work center in the schedule. The Dispatch List on the Scheduling page shows every operation with its job, work center, start, end and status — Scheduled, In Progress or Complete — so a supervisor can see the queue at each center. Time logged against an operation rolls up into the job's **Operation Time** section, where estimated hours sit next to actual hours and an efficiency percentage.\n\nGet the routing times close to right and the schedule, the cost estimate and the efficiency numbers all become trustworthy at once.\n\n### What to do first\n\n1. Open **Scheduling > Work Centers** and create one work center per machine, cell or department that takes work. Fill in daily capacity, efficiency and the two hourly rates.\n2. Open a part you make, go to its **Routing** tab and add the operations in order with a time estimate on each.\n3. Assign BOM lines to the operations that consume them.\n4. Create a job for the part and check the Dispatch List: every operation should appear on the right work center.","sections":[]}"""
