            Tags = """["manufacturing","work-orders","jobs","material-issue","variance","operations"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Work Orders: Release, Material, Operations and Variance\n\nIn Forge a work order is a **job**. The Kanban board is where jobs are created and moved through their stages; the Kanban training covers the board itself. This article is about what a job means to production once it exists: the release, the material charged to it, the operations it runs through and the cost variance you review at the end.\n\n### Release\n\nCreating a job for a part is the release. The moment the job is saved Forge does three things that make it real on the floor:\n\n- **Pins the BOM revision.** The job records which revision of the part's bill of materials it was released against. Open the job's detail panel and look for **BOM revision at release**. If engineering changes the BOM afterwards you will see a warning that the BOM has been updated since this job was released — the job still builds to the pinned revision, so nobody is surprised by a part list that changed under them.\n- **Copies the routing.** The part's operations become the job's operations, each on its work center, and they appear on the Dispatch List ready to be scheduled.\n- **Sets the estimate.** Estimated material, labor, burden and subcontract cost come from the BOM, the routing times and the work center rates. This is the number actuals are compared against later.\n\nFor an assembly with sub-assemblies you make yourself, use **Explode BOM** on the job. Forge creates a child job for each made sub-assembly, linked under the parent, so the lower-level work is released too. Child jobs show in the **Sub-Jobs** section of the parent.\n\nBecause the estimate and the BOM pin are captured at creation, get the part right first — routing, BOM, times — then create the job.\n\n### Material issues\n\nOpen a job and scroll to **Cost Analysis**. Under the cost table is the **Material Issues** list: every quantity of material charged to this job, with the part, quantity, unit cost, total, type and date. There are three types:\n\n- **Issue** — material pulled from inventory for the job. It counts toward the job's actual material cost.\n- **Return** — material sent back to stock. It reduces the job's material cost and puts the quantity back in inventory.\n- **Scrap** — material consumed and lost. It stays on the job's cost.\n\nIf material was issued and not used, click the **Return** arrow on that line. Forge writes a matching Return and adjusts both the job cost and the on-hand quantity. If the totals look stale, **Recalculate costs** rebuilds the summary from the underlying issues and time entries.\n\n### Operations\n\nA job with a routed part has one operation per routing step. Two places show them:\n\n- The **Dispatch List** on the Scheduling page, across all jobs: job number, operation, work center, scheduled start and end, hours, and a status of Scheduled, In Progress or Complete. Filter by work center to see the queue at one machine.\n- The **Operation Time** section on the job, for that job only: each operation in sequence with estimated hours, actual hours logged against it and an efficiency percentage, plus totals at the top.\n\nOperators log time against a specific operation from the shop floor kiosk or their timer, which is what fills the actual column. Efficiency well under 100 percent on one operation, job after job, usually means the routing estimate is wrong — fix it on the part and every future job gets a better estimate.\n\n### Variance review\n\nThe **Cost Analysis** table on the job is the variance review. Four cost categories — Material, Labor, Burden and Subcontract — each show **Estimated**, **Actual** and **Variance**, with a total and a variance percentage at the bottom. Variance is actual minus estimated: a positive number means the job cost more than planned and is shown as unfavorable; a negative number is favorable. Below the table, **Quoted Price** and **Actual Margin** tell you whether the job still made money.\n\nReview it when a job reaches its final stage, before the invoice goes out. Ask three questions:\n\n1. **Material over?** Check the issues list for extra issues or scrap. If the BOM was short, fix the BOM.\n2. **Labor over?** Look at Operation Time to find which operation ran long. Fix the routing time, or the process.\n3. **Subcontract over?** The vendor charged more than the operation's estimate. Update the operation or the vendor pricing.\n\nForge also watches active jobs daily and raises an event when actual cost runs more than ten percent over estimate, so a runaway job surfaces before it closes.\n\n### What to do first\n\n1. Create a job for a routed part from the Kanban board and open it. Confirm **BOM revision at release** is filled in.\n2. Open **Scheduling > Dispatch** and find the job's operations on their work centers.\n3. Have an operator log time against one operation, then check the job's **Operation Time** section.\n4. When the job reaches its last stage, read the **Cost Analysis** table and record what you learned on the part.","sections":[]}"""
        });

